  "summary": "Four November 2026 preprints apply pangenome graph references to read phasing, long-read mapping, diploid inference, and TCR germline discovery.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the replacement of a single linear reference with pangenome graphs that encode haplotype-unique variation. GRASER phases short reads directly on graph alignments using haplotype-unique edges, aiming to reduce reference bias and recover allele-specific information. DiGi applies an integer programming formulation on a haplotype trellis pangenome graph to jointly infer diploid haplotype paths from sequencing reads. The gAIRR Suite v2 analysis of 232 individuals applies pangenome analysis to the T cell receptor germline and identifies 531 novel alleles, mostly rare and enriched in African-ancestry individuals. Genomic tessellations is a different methodological contribution: a canonical DNA representation reported to speed long-read mapping and improve HG002 assembly accuracy. What is new is the shift from graph construction toward graph-based haplotype inference and germline discovery at population scale, although the tessellation preprint addresses sequence representation rather than haplotype assignment.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-19"
}
